\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The dismantling of Hong Kong has long been one of the most painful disasters in East Asia. In the past few years, the city has effectively gone up in flames: booksellers kidnapped, student protesters beaten, the free press smothered, election law amended to make sure only \u8220 patriots\u8221  can run for office. As 2021 closed out and Beijing hawks took control of the legislature, statues commemorating the Tiananmen Square massacre were being hauled off university campuses. \u8220 How did Asia\u8217 s most liberal, open and cosmopolitan city \u8230  change so fundamentally?\u8221  the activist Law asks in this philosophical memoir, written from exile in London. \u8220 How was a flourishing and free society undermined from within?\u8221 }
{\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Never an especially political child, Law remembers attending his first vigil as a teenager and weeping over the pamphlets. By the time of the famous Umbrella Movement protests in 2014, Law was a student activist, organizing peacefully for electoral reform; three years later, having won office by sweeping margins at 23, he was retroactively disqualified and thrown in prison. Back in 2014, Law writes, \u8220 riot police were deployed against a generation in Hong Kong who knew no violence\u8221 ; by 2019, the year of Hong Kong\u8217 s largest protests to date, some demonstrators carried farewell notes to their families in case they were beaten to death. Law left the next year. Today every organizer of the Umbrella Movement has been imprisoned or fled abroad, saying \u8220 Free Hong Kong\u8221  is a criminal offense and \u8220 people are being arrested for the possession of stickers.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Half memoir, half stump speech, the book often dissolves into vague encomiums to \u8220 the flame of freedom\u8221  and textbook bullet points on the rule of law. \u8220 Freedom\u8221  could have been stronger had it explored Hong Kong through the lens of Law\u8217 s own history. In the \u8217 90s, his father crossed from Guangdong into Hong Kong on a flat-bottomed boat; the rest of his family followed two years after the 1997 handover. \u8220 Since 2014 I have regularly been arrested,\u8221  Law writes calmly at one point, an understated illustration of how much has changed in the city, and might still.}
