\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Haitiwaji recited her story to the Figaro journalist Morgat, who takes certain liberties fleshing out a first-person memoir. (One doubts that Haitiwaji told her that \u8220 laughter mingled with the clink of dishes, a boisterous symphony playing over the melodies of lutes,\u8221  or that two organizations \u8220 saw their incumbent directors\u8217  terms renewed in 2018 for three and four years, respectively.\u8221 ) The book is most valuable as testimony. For Uyghurs, Haitiwaji explains, the camps are \u8220 a kind of urban legend,\u8221  made mythic by silence: \u8220 If no one talks about them, then the camps aren\u8217 t real.\u8221  Her memoir, dedicated \u8220 to all those who didn\u8217 t make it out,\u8221  contributes to a rich and painful body of memory-keeping that grows all the time.}
