\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 \u8220 \u8216 Banned in China\u8217  is too often the baseline for what is and isn\u8217 t worth reading,\u8221  the journalist Walsh observes in this lively, lucid survey of contemporary Chinese fiction. What Walsh calls \u8220 this intrusive relationship between grand and personal narratives\u8221  can reduce reading, particularly among Western audiences, to its least interesting question: Is this for or against the party? OK, but what about the characters or the plot? Walsh promises us a glimpse of something deeper: \u8220 a confusing and intricate tapestry that offers a beguiling impression of Chinese society itself.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\af2\afs24\ltrch\fs20\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Walsh covers the basics in passages on Yan Lianke, Yu Hua, Can Xue, Su Tong and other titans of the past 20 years in mainland fiction. Known for nightmarish, often lurid parables, these writers make dreamscapes where remembering is, at its worst, a prosecutable offense \u8212  in Su Tong\u8217 s \u8220 Shadow of the Hunter,\u8221  an old man \u8220 is institutionalized for digging up the streets in search of his ancestor\u8217 s bones\u8221  \u8212  or simply tiresome, a point Yan Lianke mocks in a novel where a teenager compares his work to \u8220 deserted graves.\u8221  But the reach of Chinese fiction is broad, and so, gamely, is Walsh\u8217 s. She explores alt-comics; martial arts fiction (banned in Mao\u8217 s time: How could there be vigilante warriors in a Communist utopia?); the poetry of migrant factory workers (\u8220 after it happened she / didn\u8217 t cry and didn\u8217 t / scream she just grabbed her finger / and left\u8221 ); online fantasy sagas (their heroes \u8220 shameless\u8221  and \u8220 borderline sociopathic,\u8221  Walsh notes dryly) devoured by half a billion readers; and China\u8217 s increasingly exportable sci-fi. Walsh delivers a wry cornucopia, inviting for general readers who don\u8217 t know Mo Yan from Han Han.}
